{"text": "Amara learns most successfully through explicit structured literacy instruction; pre-teaching vocabulary and morphology; models and graphic organizers; and assistive technology with direct skill teaching. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Amara may struggle with slow, effortful decoding; spelling under time pressure; copying notes while listening; and reading volume unrelated to the learning goal. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-Q-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Amara", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.525, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 5}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.5375, "end": 0.9125, "start_char": 6, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.925, "end": 1.1875, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 17}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.2, "end": 1.9125, "start_char": 18, "end_char": 30}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.925, "end": 2.15, "start_char": 31, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "explicit", "start": 2.1625, "end": 2.7875, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 47}, {"text": "structured", "start": 2.8, "end": 3.3, "start_char": 48, "end_char": 58}, {"text": "literacy", "start": 3.3125, "end": 3.7625, "start_char": 59, "end_char": 67}, {"text": "instruction", "start": 3.775, "